DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The normal relational operations (like <<, "less than") fail if  one  of
       the operands is NaN.  This will cause an exception.  To avoid this, C99
       defines the macros listed below.

       These macros are guaranteed to evaluate their arguments only once.  The
       arguments  must be of real floating‐point type (note: do not pass inte‐
       ger values as arguments to these macros, since the arguments  will  _n_o_t
       be promoted to real‐floating types).

       iissggrreeaatteerr()
              determines _(_x_) _> _(_y_) without an exception if _x or _y is NaN.

       iissggrreeaatteerreeqquuaall()
              determines _(_x_) _>_= _(_y_) without an exception if _x or _y is NaN.

       iisslleessss()
              determines _(_x_) _< _(_y_) without an exception if _x or _y is NaN.

       iisslleesssseeqquuaall()
              determines _(_x_) _<_= _(_y_) without an exception if _x or _y is NaN.

       iisslleessssggrreeaatteerr()
              determines _(_x_) _< _(_y_) _|_| _(_x_) _> _(_y_) without an exception if _x or _y
              is NaN.  This macro is not equivalent to _x _!_= _y because that ex‐
              pression is true if _x or _y is NaN.

       iissuunnoorrddeerreedd()
              determines whether its arguments are unordered, that is, whether
              at least one of the arguments is a NaN.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The macros other than iissuunnoorrddeerreedd() return the result of the relational
       comparison; these macros return 0 if either argument is a NaN.

       iissuunnoorrddeerreedd() returns 1 if _x or _y is NaN and 0 otherwise.

